    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_e3fc5398329825872e24a113fec9c7368cbcfd0f6d9344ed44e066bc9ff1ed64",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The usefulness of the survey's analytic information depends on the exact bridge between representations. Passing to a Hilbert class field preserves root discriminant, so degree-dependent discriminant bounds constrain the base field's class number; retaining ideal-class witnesses rather than only the scalar discriminant yields stronger classwise information but loses the Euler-product structure needed for a direct GRH route; and passing from a field to a defining polynomial preserves the field discriminant only when the generator gives a power basis. These contrasts identify preservation of the relevant invariant as the condition for transfer, and loss of it as the obstruction.",
