    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_f15bcca61918219b66f202e8a69d1c2bd89164047b0e077a137613ce1b0bac45",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The source separates universal test-function optimization from field-specific diagnosis. Main-term optimality does not determine the best finite-degree kernel or remainder; known small prime ideals can instead motivate kernels that weight their norms effectively; and, once a kernel and explicit-formula convention are fixed, computed ideal contributions can be subtracted from a field's deficiency to infer the residual contribution of low zeros. Local arithmetic information therefore changes both how a bound can be tailored and how excess above that bound can be attributed.",
